<p>Use this tool to help find the maximin strategy for a <m>2\times 2</m> game matrix <me>\left[\begin{matrix}
  a &amp; b\\
  c &amp; d\\
  \end{matrix}\right]</me>
with a mixed strategy equilibrium. Player 1's strategies are labeled A and B, Player 2's are C and D.</p>

<p>First enter the four values for your matrix: </p>
<p>
  <m>a=</m><mathInput name="ac"></mathInput>
  <m>b=</m><mathInput name="ad"></mathInput>
  <m>c=</m><mathInput name="bc"></mathInput>
  <m>d=</m><mathInput name="bd"></mathInput>
</p>

<p>The game matrix for Player 1's payoffs is 
<me>\left[\begin{matrix}
  $ac &amp; $ad\\
  $bc &amp; $bd\\
  \end{matrix}\right].</me></p>



 
<setup>
<line name="line1" through="(0, $ac) (1,$bc)" styleNumber="1">
  <label> C </label>
</line>
<line name="x0" through="(0, 1) (0, 2)"></line>
<line name="x1" through="(1, 1) (1, 2)"></line>

  <point name="c1"><math>(0,$ac)</math>
  <constraints>
        <constrainTo>$x0</constrainTo>
    <constrainToGrid dx="0.1" dy="0.1"/>
      </constraints>
  </point>
  <point name="c2"><math>(1,$bc)</math>
  <constraints>
        <constrainTo>$x1</constrainTo>
    <constrainToGrid dx="0.1" dy="0.1"/>
      </constraints></point>
</setup>

 


 
<setup>
<line name="line2" through="(0, $ad) (1,$bd)" styleNumber="2">
  <label>D</label>
</line>
  
    

  <point name="d1" styleNumber="2"><math>(0,$ad) </math>
  <constraints>
        <constrainTo>$x0</constrainTo>
    <constrainToGrid dx="0.1" dy="0.1"/>
      </constraints>
  </point>
  <point name="d2" styleNumber="2"><math>(1,$bd) </math>
  <constraints>
        <constrainTo>$x1</constrainTo>
    <constrainToGrid dx="0.1" dy="0.1"/>
      </constraints>
  </point>
</setup>

 


<aside><title>Graphs of both lines</title>
<p>We want to graph the lines representing Player 2's pure strategies on the same axes.</p>
<graph xmin="-0.1" xmax="1.1" ymin="-6" ymax="6" >
  $line1
  $line2
  $c1
  $c2
  $d1
  $d2
</graph>

<p>Note, if you move the endoints of the segments, it will change the payoff matrix above. To find the mixed strategy for a new matrix game, you can either fill in new entries in the matrix or adjust the graph.</p>
</aside>

<p>
  Fill in the slope and intercept for line C:
  <m>y=</m><mathInput name="userslopeC"/><m>x + </m><mathInput name="userinterC"/>.
  <answer name="ansC">
    <award name="correct2">
      <when>$userslopeC = $bc-$ac and $userinterC = $ac</when>
    </award></answer>
</p>

<p>
  Fill in the slope and intercept for line D:
  <m>y=</m><mathInput name="userslopeD"/><m>x + </m><mathInput name="userinterD"/>.
  <answer name="ansD">
    <award name="correct3">
      <when>$userslopeD = $bd-$ad and $userinterD = $ad</when>
    </award></answer>
</p>


<setup>
  <math name="xcoor" simplify>($ad - $ac)/($bc - $ac - $bd +$ad)</math>
  <math name="ycoor" simplify>($bc- $ac)*$xcoor +$ac</math>
  <math name="probA" simplify>1-$xcoor</math>
  <math name="probB" >$xcoor</math>
  <math name="payoff1" >$ycoor</math>
</setup>



<p>The intersection of the two lines is a point of the form <m>(p, m)</m> where <m>p</m> is the probability that Player 1 plays B, and <m>m</m> is the payoff to Player 1.</p>

<p>The intersection point is <math>($xcoor, $ycoor)</math>.</p>

<p>Using our intersection point, we know Player 1 should play A with a probability of 
<answer><award>$probA</award></answer> and play B with a probability of <answer><award>$probB</award></answer>.</p>
<p>The expected payoff to Player 1 is <answer><award>$payoff1</award></answer></p>